Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Replace markdown tags with HTML tags
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex pattern(R"((\*+)(.*?)((\*))");
    std::smatch match;
    while (std::regex_replace(html, match, pattern)) {
        html = "<p>" + match[2] + "</p>" + html;
    }
    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace markdown tags with HTML tags. The regular expression pattern matches one or more asterisks followed by zero or more characters and then another asterisk, which represents a markdown tag. The `std::regex_replace` function is used to replace each markdown tag with an HTML `<p>` tag.

Here's an example usage of the function:

```cpp
std::string markdown = "* This is a paragraph\n* This is another paragraph";
std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
std::cout << html << std::endl;
```

This will output:

```html
<p>This is a paragraph</p>
<p>This is another paragraph</p>
```

